  "narrative": {
    "intro": "New Jersey federal contract obligations totaled $7.8B in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 370,349 awards at an average award size of $21K, and obligations fell 16.5% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $5.1B obligated, and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION is the largest vendor with $2.2B in obligations. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $1.7B. Other notable agency totals include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $856M and the Department of Homeland Security at $605M. Construction and professional services show significant activity, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $500M and Computer Systems Design Services at $494M. Historical context shows higher annual totals in prior fiscal years, including $9.8B in FY24 and $9.3B in FY22 and FY25. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial and fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are federal obligations across agencies in New Jersey?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense dominates federal buying with $5.1B in obligations for FY2026 to date. The next largest agencies are the Department of Veterans Affairs with $856M and the Department of Homeland Security with $605M. The top ten buying agencies together include transportation, health, energy, and civilian procurement totaling several additional hundreds of millions each."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors drive contracting in New Jersey?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences leads industries with $1.7B in obligations. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing follows with $1B, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is $500M. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION is the largest vendor at $2.2B, followed by several firms with obligations in the low hundreds of millions."
      }
    ]
  },
